====================(Nginx)=============================
NGINX (pronounced "Engine-X") is:
* A web server refers to both the physical machine and software
  running on the machine 
* Primary function is to serve web pages to client browser's
* A web server that serves content like websites and applications.
* A reverse proxy that sits between clients (like browsers) and backend servers (like application servers or databases).
* A load balancer that distributes traffic across multiple servers to ensure reliability and speed.
  ==> some load balancing method :
    * Least Connections.
    * Round Robin.
* A proxy server that helps with caching, security, and handling client requests.
Proxy: 
==> SSL/ TLS:
    Secures communication between client and server using HTTPS.
==> Caching:
    Storing responses temporarily to improve performance and reduce server load.


=================(installing nginx)==================
* sudo apt update
* sudo apt install nginx
* sudo systemctl start nginx
* sudo systemctl enable nginx
* sudo systemctl status nginx


====================(Configuring NGINX as Web Server)===========================
http {
    server {
    }
    
    server {
    }
}
* it is possible to add multiple server directives into the http context 
  to define multiple virtual servers
* the server configuration block usually includes a listen directive to 
  specify the IP address and port, on which the serve listens for requests, 
  Both IPv4 or IPv6 are accepted.
* if the listen directive is not included at all :
        ==> The standard port is 80/tcp.
        ==> The default port is 8000/tcp
* localhost: refers to the local machine that you are currently using 
* worker_processes:
  instead of using a new processs for every incoming connection, Nginx uses
  worker processes that handle many connections using a single-threaded event loop.
  ==> it's generally recommended to set the worker processes value to be equal to
      the number of CPU cores that is available on the server where Nginx is running 
  ==> worker_processes auto : means nginx automatically detects the number of CPU cores 
      available on the server and starts a corresponding number of worler nodes. 
* worker_connections:
  Per worker process: how many simultaneous connections can be opened
events {
  worker_connections 1024;
}
* if we have 1 worker processes, you will be able to serve 1024 clients
* if we have 2 worker processes you will be able to serve 2*1024 = 2048 clients
* server_name : which domain or IP address this server block should respond to, 
                basically where the client sending those requests
* configuring locations :
  there are two types of paramater to the location directive:
      --> Prefix strings (pathnames) : /some/path
      --> Regular expressions : ~ or ~*

* error_log /var/log/nginx/error.log warn;
  Defines where error logs are stored 
  warn sets the logging level(debug, info, warn, error..)
  Full path: /var/log/nginx/error.log
* pid /var/run/nginx.pid;
  Stores the process Id of the main NGINX process
  used for stopping/restarting NGINX
* Handle PHP files : instructions n how to handle requests for PHP files: 
  ~ regular expressions
  \.php$ : mathces anu URI ending in .php like example.com/page.php
  * fastcgi_pass unix:/var/run/php/php-fpm.sock;
      -tells NGINX where to send PHP requests 
      -uses a unix socket file for communication (faster than TCP)
      -PHP-FMP(fastCGI Process Manager) listens to this socket 
      -Alternative TCP format would be: fastcgi_pass 127.0.0.1:9000;
      ---> * this says "send PHP requests to a program called PHP_FPM"
           * PHP_FPM: it's like a helper that knows how to process PHP code.
           * unix:/var/run/php/php-fpm.sock: this is the path to the socket file
                which is like a telephone line that nginx uses to talk to PHP-FPM 
  * fastcgi_index index.php;
     -sets default PHP file to index.php where directory is requested
     -if user visits example.com/blog/ it will try to serve blog/index.php
  * fastcgi_param SCRIPT_FILENAME $document_root$fastcgi_script_name;
    -Contructs th full path to the PHP SCRIPT_FILENAME
    -$document_root is your site's root directory.
    -$fastcgi_script_name is the requested PHP file
    -example: if root is /var/www/html and request is for /blog/post.php
          $document_root = /var/www/html
          $fastcgi_script_name = /blog/post.php
          Final path = /var/www/html/blog/post.php
    -----> * this tells PHP-FPM where the php file is on your server
           * $document_root: this is where your website files are stored(the 
              base folder for your website)
           * $fastcgi_script_name : this is the specific PHP file that the user is asking for
           * Together if forms the full path to the PHP
                like: /var/www/html/index.php
  * include fastcgi_params:
    -includes standard fastCGI paramaters from /etc/nginx/fastcgi_params
    -contains essentil parameters like:
        - QUERY_STRING (for GET parameters);
        - REQUEST_METHOD   (GET, POST, ...)
        - CONTENT_TYPE
        - CONTENT_LENGTH
        - server environment variables.
    ---> * This includes a list of extra settings (stored in the fastcgi_params file).
         * These settings help Nginx and PHP-FPM work smoothly together,
           like passing environment variables or headers.
     ===> Process flow when a PHP file is requested:
        1- User requests example.om/page.php
        2- NGINX matches the .php pattern.
        3- NGINX passes request to PHP_FPM via the socket
        4- PHP_FPM processes the PHP file
        5- Result is sent back to NGINX
        6- NGINX sends the response to the user.
